**Verdict: PASS.** I raised one new finding, **F-DG3-100 (Low, not a mandatory violation)**. Nothing Critical, High or mandatory was found. One check (SEC-R1-21) is recorded as FAIL because of that finding. In DG2 rounds 14 and 15, Low findings sat alongside a PASS verdict in the same way. The gate validator rejects a record with a non-PASS check, though, so this record will not count towards DG3 until F-DG3-100 is fixed and verified in a later round.

HEAD was `ec45ef1`, not `928b7654`, so I checked before going on. The difference is only the round-1 freeze files under `docs/delivery/`, and the recomputed candidate ID matched (`sha256:873115d9…`, 753 files).

**Required checks.** All of these ran and exited 0:
- build, typecheck, lint, the OpenAPI lint (270 operations), the no-CDN check and the format check;
- unit tests: 1528 of 1528 passed on Node 22 and on Node 24;
- integration tests: 791 of 791 passed twice, once with the locale unset and once with `LANG=C.UTF-8`, each on a throwaway PostgreSQL. This covered migrations 0001 to 0027 and `contract.test.ts` with all 270 operations live; all eight `p3-pending` lists are empty;
- the validator against DG2 and DG1 (historical mode): PASS.

**Disclosed in the record:**
- My first integration attempt exited 126 before any test ran: my database helper script wasn't executable. I re-ran it; both logs are kept.
- The dependency-verification script (`pnpm deps:verify`) needs the live package registry. It printed nothing for 300 s and I killed it. That is the known live-registry residual (D-057).
- Two warnings in the logs were already there in DG2: a Vite chunk-size advisory and a Fastify deprecation notice.

**My own probes** (sources and logs under `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG3/code-security/round-1/`):
- **Read-only auditor sweep.** It is generated from the contract and covers all 64 P3 write operations. Every call that names a record got 403 with only an `authorization.denied` audit event. All 29 P3 tables were unchanged before and after.
- **Below the API, as the runtime database role:**
  - the database refused all six separation-of-duties bypasses, each with its own named constraint;
  - the runtime role cannot switch the guards off;
  - in 25 races of A→B against B→A, exactly one commit landed every time;
  - a 3-cycle, an un-archived edge and a re-pointed edge were each refused, with the cycle named;
  - weight totals of 95%, 105% and 99.99% were refused with nothing written;
  - acting on someone's behalf was refused on the approvals whose schemas don't accept it (weight-set approval, both Finance validations, override revoke);
  - all 282 audit events had the `{from, to}` changes shape.
- **Formula engine under hostile input.** It never threw, never evaluated anything outside its grammar, gave Unknown on division by zero, and matched an independent exact calculation on 2,853 random expressions.

**F-DG3-100.** ADR-0024 §6 says an ESLint rule enforces "no dynamic code" in the formula engine, backed by a source-scan test. Both still accept these forms, which do run code from a string:
- `Reflect.construct(Function, …)` and `Reflect.apply(Function, …)`;
- an aliased `Function` (`const F = Function; F("…")`);
- a computed `["constructor"](…)`;
- `createRequire(…)("vm")`.

With these placed in `evaluate.ts` in a throwaway clone, ESLint exited 0 and the scan passed. The engine as shipped contains no dynamic code, so this is a gap in the guard, not an exploitable defect. The owner is kpi-benefits-engineer.

**Observations, not raised:**
- **G4 authorization.** The G4 submit and decide routes reuse DG2's gate routes, which authorize with the caller identity loaded when the request arrives. They don't re-check it at commit time. Every one of the 109 P3 operations does.
- **G4 and deliverable delegation.** ADR-0021 §6 deliberately lets someone decide on the owner's behalf for the G4 decision and deliverable acceptance. Neither lets the submitter decide.

**Files written:**
- `docs/delivery/reviews/DG3/round-1/code-security-reviewer.json` (checked against the review schema)
- `code-security-reviewer.findings.json` (checked against the findings schema)
- `code-security-reviewer.md` (narrative)

I couldn't remove the run's own scratch files under `$TMPDIR`: a safety check blocked the removal. All the clones were deleted, and the runner clears that directory when the run ends.
